# The Face - a shot firer, three shots a day, and a charge that does not go off.
# Written for the library. One person, one rule, and the question of who the rule is for.

title: The Face
start: start

== start ==
bg east_corner
music room_tone
The quarry has four benches and a face that has been worked from the top down since
nineteen fifty-one, and it closes on Friday, which is four days.
Three shots a day: eleven, two, and four. You load them yourself, you fire them
yourself, and you are the only person on the site who is allowed to push the handle.
-> the_morning

== the_morning ==
The eleven o'clock goes up clean and the men come back off the bank at half past and
the loader takes the spoil away and it is a Tuesday like any other Tuesday.
At two you load the second one into the same bench, eight holes, and you are thinking
about the fact that there are four days left and that after Friday nobody will be
allowed up here at all.
-> the_second

== the_second ==
The whistle goes. The men walk back to the bothy and one of them, the lad who started
in the spring, stops at the corner and looks back, which they do for the first month.
You wait for the count. You always count.
-> the_count

== the_count ==
Eight. Seven. Six. You count them down and at three you push the handle and the ground
moves the way it always moves, all eight going within a second of each other, and the
dust comes up over the bench and hangs there.
And then, in the quiet afterwards, there is one more. Not a bang. A click, from up at
the face, the sound a hole makes when it has not done anything at all.
-> the_misfire

== the_misfire ==
The rule is thirty minutes. It is written on the wall of the magazine in your own
handwriting and it says: in the event of a misfire, do not approach the face for thirty
minutes, and then approach it as though the charge is live.
The men are in the bothy with the kettle on. The owner is at the gate in his car
because he comes at four. The lad is still at the corner looking up.
-> the_decision

== the_decision ==
You have the handle in your hand and the box is open and there is nothing to do for
twenty-nine minutes except stand here and be a person who is waiting.
* Wait the thirty. It is written on the wall. -> wait
* Go up now. You have done this nineteen years. -> go_up
* Send them all home and come back to it in the morning. -> send_home
* Walk up to the bothy and tell them what a misfire is. -> tell_them

== wait ==
You put the handle down and you sit on the box and you watch the face, and the dust
settles, and at eleven minutes the lad comes over and stands next to you without saying
anything and stays there for the rest of it.
At thirty you walk up the bank. The hole is where you left it, and it is live, and you
deal with it the way the book says, and it takes another forty minutes and nobody is
hurt and the day is gone.
-> ending_waited

== go_up ==
You walk up the bank at nine minutes, because the owner's car is at the gate and the
lorry is booked and because you have done this nineteen years and you know what a
misfire sounds like.
The hole has already gone. The stemming is blown out and the rock is broken and there
is a fresh scar on the face that was not there when you counted, and it went at some
point while you were standing at the box with the handle in your hand, and you did not
hear it because it went without a sound.
-> ending_went_quiet

== send_home ==
You walk to the bothy and tell them to pack up, and the lad asks whether it is because
of the click, and you say yes, and he nods like somebody who has been told a fact rather
than a decision.
They go. The owner's car is still at the gate and you walk down and tell him through
the window that the face is live and nobody goes up until it is dealt with, and he asks
how long, and you say you do not know, and he drives off without saying anything else.
-> ending_handed_on

== tell_them ==
You put the handle in the box and you walk to the bothy, and four men look up, and you
tell them what a misfire is: that the charge is in the hole, that it is live, that the
rule is thirty minutes, and that the clicking is not the charge failing, it is the
charge deciding.
The lad asks what happens if it goes while you are up there. You tell him the truth,
which is that you do not go up while it is deciding, and that this is the whole of the
rule and that it is written on the wall because it is the whole of the rule.
-> the_telling

== the_telling ==
You sit down with them and drink the tea, which you have not done in nineteen years,
and at thirty minutes you go up with the lad at the bottom of the bank watching, and you
deal with it, and it is the same as it would have been if you had gone up at nine
minutes, and that is not the point and you both know it.
-> ending_told

== ending_waited ==
The face is dealt with and the lorry comes on Thursday instead and the owner says
nothing about it, and on Friday the quarry closes and the men take their tools and the
lad takes the whistle, because you gave it to him, because there is nobody else.
You have the rule on the wall of the magazine in your own handwriting and the magazine
is going to be filled in on Monday, and you take the piece of wall with the rule on it
out with a bolster and a hammer, which takes an hour, and which nobody asks you about.
-> END

== ending_went_quiet ==
You stand at the top of the bank with the box in your hand and the scar in front of you
and you work out, from where the stemming has landed, that it went at about four
minutes, which is three minutes after the click and five minutes before you started
walking.
You write it in the book as a misfire that fired. The inspector reads the book on
Thursday and asks you one question, which is how long you waited, and you tell him nine
minutes, and he writes that down, and that is the last anybody says about it.
-> END

== ending_handed_on ==
The face is dealt with on Thursday by a man from the company who comes with a van and a
certificate, and he asks you why it was not dealt with on Tuesday, and you tell him the
truth, which is that you sent four men home rather than stand at the box for thirty
minutes, and he writes it down and does not look up.
The quarry closes on Friday and the lad goes to a site in the next county and you do not
hear from him again, and the rule is still on the wall of the magazine when they fill
the magazine in, in your handwriting, in ink that has not faded at all.
-> END

== ending_told ==
You have never told anybody what a misfire is. Nineteen years, and the rule was a thing
you kept rather than a thing you said, and saying it out loud in a bothy with four men
and a kettle is the only piece of the job you are going to miss.
On Friday the lad asks whether he can have the whistle and you say yes, and he asks
whether the rule is the same everywhere and you say no, and he asks what he should do
then, and you say the thing that is actually true: that he should write his own on the
wall and then keep it.
-> END
